## DESCRIPTION
## Applied problem that asks for total number of baked goods depending on the 
## number of ingredients on hand. 
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS('linear system', 'application', 'bakery', 'cookies', 'cakes', 'muffins')

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Systems of linear equations)
## DBsection(Applications)
## Date(September 5, 2012)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)

# updated 2023.11.02

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'randomPerson.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$cookies = random(2, 6);
$cakes   = random(2, 6);
$muffins = random(1, 5);

$A = Matrix([ map { [ random(2, 8), random(1, 5), random(2, 6) ] } (1 .. 3) ]);

$total = $A * Vector($cookies, $cakes, $muffins);

$flour = Matrix($total)->element(1);
$sugar = Matrix($total)->element(2);
$eggs = Matrix($total)->element(3);

$p = randomPerson();

# Now begin with the Problem Statement

BEGIN_PGML

On Fridays, the Peace Frog bakery makes cookies, cakes and muffins.  The following table
shows the ingredient amounts needed for each baked good with flour and sugar measured in cups and eggs is number of:

[#
    [. .][.flour .][.sugar.][.eggs.]*
    [.cookies.][.[$A->element(1,1)].][.[$A->element(1,2)].][.[$A->element(3,1)].]*
    [.cakes. .][.[$A->element(1,2)].][.[$A->element(2,2)].][.[$A->element(3,2)].]*
    [.muffin .][.[$A->element(1,3)].][.[$A->element(2,3)].][.[$A->element(3,3)].]*

#]

(Each require other ingredients as well, but don’t worry about it). [$p]
the Baker looks over [$p->their] inventory and 
realizes that [$p->they] has [$flour] cups of ﬂour, 
[$sugar] cups of sugar and [$eggs] eggs. 
How many of each type of product (cookies,
cakes, muffins) should [$p] make to use all the ingredients?

- Number of batches of cookies: [_]{Real($cookies)}
- Number of cakes: [_]{Real($cakes)}
- Number of batches of muffins: [_]{Real($muffins)}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First, we will define the following varibles
[```
\begin{aligned}
x & = \text{number of batches of cookies} \\
y &= \text{number of cakes } \\
z & = \text{number of batches of muffins} 
\end{aligned}
```]

We use the table given above to calculate how much flour, sugar and eggs are used respectively.  This becomes:
[```\begin{aligned}
[$A->element(1,1)] x + [$A->element(1,2)] y + [$A->element(1,3)] z & = [$flour] \\
[$A->element(2,1)] x + [$A->element(2,2)] y + [$A->element(2,3)] z & = [$sugar] \\
[$A->element(3,1)] x + [$A->element(3,2)] y + [$A->element(3,3)] z & = [$eggs]  \\
\end{aligned}```]

Then the standard way (although there are others) is to write these equations in augmented
matrix form or
[```
\left[\begin{array}{rrr|r}
[$A->element(1,1)] & [$A->element(1,2)] & [$A->element(1,3)] & [$flour] \\
[$A->element(2,1)] & [$A->element(2,2)] & [$A->element(2,3)] & [$sugar] \\
[$A->element(3,1)] & [$A->element(3,2)] & [$A->element(3,3)] & [$eggs] \\
\end{array}\right] ```]

Then using Gauss-Jordon reduction use row operations to put this matrix in row-reduced
echelon form or
[```
\left[\begin{array}{rrr|r}
1 & 0 & 0 & [$cookies] \\
0 & 1 & 0 & [$cakes] \\
0 & 0 & 1 & [$muffins] \\
\end{array}\right] ```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
